# Semantic HTML

In short, semantic HTML is all about the **right choice of HTML elements.** 

In the previous section on the basic webpage structure we already got a glimpse of what's possible with semantic HTML (remember the browser's reading mode?). Here, we revise some other important aspects of semantic HTML.

</ExerciseBridge>


<ExerciseCard question={getQuestion('semantic-definition')} />


<ExerciseCard question={getQuestion('html-full-of-divs')} />


<ExerciseBridge title="It's about accessibility" size="wide">

Consider, what a website communicates even without its visual styling. After all, semantic HTML is an important **key element for making a website accessible**. 

- For humans, with their different needs and interests.
- And for machines, be it screen readers, printers, web crawlers, search engines, ...

</ExerciseBridge>


<ExerciseBridge title="How Mozilla puts it:" size="wide">

<q>HTML tags must be used for their semantic value, not their appearance. It's always possible to totally change the look and feel of a given tag using CSS so, when using HTML, take the time to focus on the meaning rather than the appearance.</q> 

[&mdash; MDN Guide on HTML](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Guides/Cheatsheet)

</ExerciseBridge>


<ExerciseBridge title="This is a core take away. Please memorise!">

But now, let's turn to visual styling once again. If you carefully read the feedbacks above, you should already know the answer.

</ExerciseBridge>


<ExerciseCard question={getQuestion('html-without-css')} />



<ExerciseBridge title="DH Focus: Semantic Dates and Times", size="wide">

With semantic markup you can go really deep. Highly relevant, especially for DH related matters, is how to mark up dates and times.

Imagine you want to publish a heterogenous dataset where dates and times occur in various formats. [This is](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Structuring_content/Advanced_text_features#marking_up_times_and_dates) how you would encode the dates as unambiguous, semantic HTML, while preserving the source formatting in the displayed text.

</ExerciseBridge>



